THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
SRC_001 is incomplete (not merely limited): mutual recursion between argument attacks and warrant attacks is required to preserve the 'reinstatement as a derived property' guarantee in the expanded semantics. Suppose warrant W attacks argument X, and argument A attacks warrant W. In the inner pass (warrants only), W has no attackers and is accepted; in the outer pass (arguments only), A has no attackers and is accepted, so X is refuted by the already-accepted W. But A's successful attack on W should reinstate X by defeating its only attacker. Since the inner pass never sees A (an argument, evaluated only in the outer pass), W stays accepted and X stays refuted. Reinstatement of X therefore fails to be derived, violating the stated guarantee.

TARGET:
Two-level warrant validity: split attack edges into provisional and committed based on an inner grounded labelling of a warrant graph. First compute grounded extension on the subgraph of warrants only; then construct the outer attack relation using only attacks whose warrant node is accepted; finally compute the grounded extension on that derived graph. This gives a compositional, polynomial two-pass semantics and preserves root validity because roots are untouched by warrant filtering. Weakness: the inner and outer labellings are not mutually recursive; an attack on a warrant by an argument that is only accepted in the outer labelling is invisible to the inner pass, so some self-referential challenges to warrants are missed.